# uv-manager — example site settings
#
# Everything here is a real, implemented environment variable. Source this
# from a profile.d script, copy the relevant lines into your Lmod modulefile
# as setenv() calls, or use it as a checklist. Nothing here is required: a
# site that exports a conventional $SCRATCH needs no configuration at all.
#
# Everything the wrapper reads for itself is spelled UVM_*. The UV_* names
# below are uv's own configuration, included because these are the settings
# that actually matter on a parallel filesystem and are easy to overlook.
# Five more — the storage locations — are exported by the wrapper on the
# executing node and override anything set for them here.


# ===========================================================================
# 1. Where per-user uv state lives  (the one setting most sites need)
# ===========================================================================
#
# UVM_ROOT is architecture-NEUTRAL. The wrapper appends the architecture at
# exec time, so it is safe to set this on a login node and inherit it onto
# compute nodes of a different architecture.
#
# It needs to be:
#   - large (managed interpreters and wheel caches are tens of GB)
#   - generous on inodes (a populated tree is easily 10^5 files)
#   - visible from every node that will run uv, at the same path
#   - not the home directory
#
# If unset, the wrapper tries, in order:
#     $CLUSTER_SCRATCH  $RCAC_SCRATCH  $SCRATCH  $PSCRATCH  $WORK  $PROJECT
# appending "/.uv" to the first that is an existing, writable directory, and
# refusing to run if none is. There is deliberately no /tmp fallback.

# Purdue RCAC community clusters:
#export UVM_ROOT="${CLUSTER_SCRATCH}/.uv"

# Purdue Anvil. RCAC's own guidance points long-lived Python
# environments at $PROJECT rather than $SCRATCH, because scratch is purged
# after 30 days and uv does not detect a partially purged environment.
#export UVM_ROOT="${PROJECT}/.uv"

# NERSC Perlmutter:
#export UVM_ROOT="${PSCRATCH}/.uv"

# TACC — $WORK is not purged, $SCRATCH is:
#export UVM_ROOT="${WORK}/.uv"

# OLCF (per-allocation rather than per-user):
#export UVM_ROOT="${MEMBERWORK}/<projid>/.uv"


# ===========================================================================
# 2. Version policy
# ===========================================================================

# Pin the uv version. Recommended for anything automation depends on —
# notably a Globus Compute endpoint provisioning environments to match a
# client's SDK versions. A pin selects which installed version `current`
# points at, so switching between already-downloaded versions is instant.
#export UVM_PIN=0.12.2

# Mirror base for the installer, for sites with no egress. The wrapper
# fetches <base>/install.sh, or <base>/<version>/install.sh when pinned.
#export UVM_INSTALL_URL=https://mirror.example.edu/astral/uv


# ===========================================================================
# 3. Provisioning behaviour
# ===========================================================================

# Seconds to wait for another process's provisioning lock.
#export UVM_LOCK_TIMEOUT=180

# Seconds after which an untouched lock is assumed abandoned and broken.
# Guards against a holder killed by SIGKILL, job cancellation or node failure.
#export UVM_LOCK_STALE=600

# Override the architecture key. Default is `uname -m`. Set this if login and
# compute images differ in a way `uname -m` does not capture — glibc version
# skew, musl, or x86-64 microarchitecture levels. Keep it cheap to compute:
# the wrapper evaluates it on every single invocation. Compute it on the
# executing node — a login node's value, inherited as a literal through
# `sbatch --export=ALL`, sends the wrapper and every trampoline into another
# node's tree.
#export UVM_PLATFORM="$(uname -m)-glibc$(getconf GNU_LIBC_VERSION | awk '{print $2}')"


# ===========================================================================
# 4. uv's own settings that matter on a parallel filesystem
# ===========================================================================
#
# The wrapper does not set any of these. They are site or user policy, and
# changing uv's documented defaults for everyone has its own costs. Set them
# deliberately if they apply to you.
#
# Prefer uv's system configuration file, /etc/uv/uv.toml, where you can: it
# MERGES with the user's ~/.config/uv/uv.toml rather than replacing it.
# Do NOT use UV_CONFIG_FILE for site defaults — that replaces user config
# entirely. Environment variables are the fallback when you cannot manage
# /etc on compute node images.

# Link mode. uv defaults to `clone` (copy-on-write reflink), which Lustre,
# GPFS and NFS do not support; it then tries hardlink, which fails across
# filesystems. With the cache under UVM_ROOT and a project's .venv on home,
# users see "Failed to hardlink files; falling back to full copy" on every
# install. `copy` makes that explicit and silences the warning. The real fix
# is keeping the venv on the same filesystem as the cache.
#export UV_LINK_MODE=copy

# Precompile bytecode at install time. Without this, the first import from
# every rank of a large job writes .pyc files concurrently into the same
# directory, which is a metadata storm on Lustre and an occasional source of
# hard-to-diagnose races.
#export UV_COMPILE_BYTECODE=1

# Wheel builds honour TMPDIR. Building torch or mpi4py from source in a small
# tmpfs /tmp fails with an error that does not point at the cause.
#export TMPDIR=/scratch/$USER/tmp

# Cap concurrency. uv defaults to roughly one install thread per core; on a
# 128-core login node that is more metadata traffic than a shared filesystem
# should have to absorb.
#export UV_CONCURRENT_INSTALLS=8
#export UV_CONCURRENT_DOWNLOADS=16

# Behind a TLS-inspecting proxy, uv's bundled Mozilla root store rejects the
# substituted certificate. Use the system trust store instead.
#export UV_SYSTEM_CERTS=1

# Internal package index.
#export UV_DEFAULT_INDEX=https://mirror.example.edu/pypi/simple

# uv waits this long for its own flock-based locks. If your scratch
# filesystem has flock disabled entirely, uv fails outright with
# "Function not implemented (os error 38)" and there is no bypass. Test it.
#export UV_LOCK_TIMEOUT=300


# ===========================================================================
# 5. Guidance worth giving users, not setting for them
# ===========================================================================
#
# Project environments (`uv sync`, `uv run`) still default to ./.venv, which
# the wrapper deliberately does not relocate. Tell users to keep projects off
# /home, and to give each architecture its own environment:
#
#     export UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT=".venv-$(uname -m)"
#
# For many-rank jobs, resolve the environment once in the prologue and launch
# the interpreter directly rather than putting `uv run` on the hot path —
# `uv run` takes a lock on the project environment.
